/*! \page scft_propagator_page Appendix: Modified Diffusion Equations

\ref scft_thin_films_page  (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref psfts_page  (Next)
<BR>

Below, we describe the self-consistent field theory (SCFT) equations
that are solved by PSCF, giving a complete overview of the mathematical
system of equations that are solved by the software and summarizing
their connection to corresponding physical properties. This is not
intended to be an introduction to the field theory itself, and we do
not provide a derivation of the SCFT equations. The presentation
assumes some familiarity with polymer physics and statistical
mechanics.

\section scft_propagator_setup_sec Model and notation

PSCF solves the SCFT equations for an incompressible mixture of any number
of block polymer species and point-like solvent molecular species.

In what follows, we consider a system with \f$ C \f$ distinct monomer
types, \f$ P \f$ block polymer species, \f$ S\f$ solvent species. We use
a convention in which the symbols \f$ i, j = 0, \ldots, C-1\f$ are used
as indices for monomer types, symbols \f$ m, n \f$ are used as indices
for blocks within a specific block polymer species, and symbols
\f$ a, b\f$ are used as indices for molecular species indices.  In these
notes, we use a convention in which species indices are ordered with all
polymeric species listed first, so that species index values in the range
\f$ a, b = 0, \ldots, P-1\f$ denote polymeric species, and values in the
range \f$ P,\ldots, P+S-1 \f$ denote solvent species.  A zero-based
indexing scheme for indices is used throughout the notes to facilitate
comparison to C/C++ source code.

To characterize inhomogeneous states, we consider the average number
concentration \f$\langle c_{i}({\bf r}) \rangle\f$ for monomers of each
type \f$ i \f$ at each position \f$ {\bf r} \f$. In a model for an
effectively incompressible liquid in which each monomer is assumed to
occupy a volume \f$ v \f$, we may define a non-dimensional monomer
concentration
\f[
  \phi_{i}(\textbf{r}) = v \langle c_{i}({\bf r}) \rangle
  \quad,
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (1)
\f]
for each monomer type, where \f$ v \f$ is a monomer reference volume
that. By convention, we required that \f$ v \f$ has the same value for
all monomer types. The non-dimensional monomer concentration fields
\f$ \phi_{0}, \ldots, \phi_{C-1} \f$ are referred to elsewhere in the
PSCF documentation as "c fields".

Incompressibility is imposed at a mean-field level by requiring that
\anchor scft_propagator_incompressibility_eqn
\f[
   1 = \sum_{i=0}^{C-1} \phi_{i}({\bf r})
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (2)
\f]
at every position \f$ {\bf r} \f$ within the problem domain. In
such an incompressible liquid, each of the non-dimensional monomer
concentration fields may also be interpreted as local volume fraction
for the specified monomer type. These "c fields" are thus also
sometimes referred to a volume fraction fields.

\section scft_propagator_scf_sec Self-consistent field approximation

SCFT for a liquid of flexible polymers is based on a mean-field
approximation that allows us to approximately predict properties of
an interacting liquid by considering the behavior of a corresponding
inhomogeneous gas of noninteracting molecules. We refer to this
hypothetical non-interacting gas as the ideal-gas reference system.
The ideal gas reference system must have the same chemical composition
as the real system of interest and an inhomogeneous average concenration
\f$ \langle c_{i}({\bf r}) \rangle \f$ for each monomer type
\f$ i \f$ that is equal to that in the real system.
Inhomogeneous monomer concentrations are induced in this reference
system by a set of potential energy fields that couple to different
types of monomer: Each monomer of type \f$ i \f$ at position
\f$ {\bf r} \f$ within this ideal gas is subjected to a potential
\f$ k_{B}Tw_{i}(\textbf{r}) \f$. In a self-consistent field
approximation, this potential represents a free energy cost for
placing a monomer of a specified type at a specified position, due
to interactions among monomers within an inhomogeneous structure.

In SCFT, the monomer chemical potential fields (or "w fields")
\f$ w_{0}, \ldots, w_{C-1} \f$ at each position \f$ {\bf r} \f$ are assumed
to depend upon monomer volume fraction fields near that point in a manner
that reflects the mixing thermodynamics of the types of monomer present in
a mixture.  PSCF currently uses a simple approximation for the dependence
of the w fields on local composition that is based on an inhomogeneous
generalization of the model for excess free energy used in the
Flory-Huggins theory of homogeneous polymer mixtures. In this
approximation, a solution to the SCFT problem is required to satisfy the
self-consistent field (SCF) equations
\anchor scft_propagator_scf_eqn
\f[
   w_{i}({\bf r})
   = \sum_{j=0}^{C-1} \chi_{ij} \phi_{j}({\bf r})
   + \xi({\bf r})
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (3)
\f]
for all \f$ i = 0,\ldots,C-1 \f$, where \f$ \chi_{ij}\f$ is a
dimensionless binary Flory-Huggins interaction parameter for monomer types
\f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$, defined such that \f$ \chi_{ij} = \chi_{ji} \f$.
Here, \f$ \xi({\bf r}) \f$ is a dimensionless Lagrange multiplier
pressure field that must be chosen such that the monomer concentrations
in the ideal gas reference system satisfy the
\ref scft_propagator_incompressibility_eqn "incompressibility constraint"
given above.

In SCFT, average monomer concentration and volume fraction fields are
computed by computing these properties for the ideal gas reference system.
An iterative solution of an SCFT problem thus starts with an initial guess
for the w fields for all monomer types, followed by iterative adjustment
of these fields until the w fields and corresponding c fields satisfy the
SCF equations and the incompressibility constraint.

\section scft_propagator_graph_sec Branched Polymer Topology

Each polymer species in a system modeled by PSCF must be a block polymer
consisting of one or more blocks connected at junction points.
Homopolymers are treated as a special case of a block polymer with
only one block.  Let \f$ B_{a} \f$ denote the number of blocks in block
polymer species number \f$ a \f$, for \f$ a = 0, \ldots, P-1 \f$.


Each block within a block polymer connects two vertices. Each such vertex
is either a junction to which two or more blocks are connected or a chain
end to which only one block is attached.  In the language of graph theory,
every allowed polymer structure corresponds to an acyclic connected graph
(also known as a "tree") with some number of "edges" (or blocks) and
"nodes" (or vertices).  PSCF can only handle polymers with the topology
of a connected acyclic graph, in which there is exactly one path (i.e.,
sequence of connected edges) connecting any two vertices.  A standard
theorem of graph theory aserts that any connected acyclic graph with
\f$ B_{a} \f$ edges must have exactly \f$ B_{a} + 1 \f$ vertices or
nodes. An acyclic block polymer with \f$ B_{a} \f$ blocks thus always has
exactly \f$ B_{a} + 1 \f$ vertices (i.e., junctions and free chain ends).
For example, a linear triblock terpolymer with three blocks in a row
has four vertices (two free ends and two junctions), and so does a star
triblock copolymer with three blocks that meet at a central junction
(three free ends and one junction).

PSCF assigns each block within a polymer species \f$ a \f$ an integer
index in the range \f$ [0, \ldots, B_{a}-1 ]\f$, where \f$ B_{a} \f$
is the number of blocks in that species. Each of the \f$ B_{a} + 1 \f$
vertices within such a species (i.e., each junction or chain end) is
assigned an integer index in the range \f$ [ 0, \ldots, B_{a} ] \f$.
The structure of a block polymer can thus be characterized by assigning
each block a monomer type, a contour length, and a pair of integer ids
for the two vertices to which the block is attached. The length of each
block is given by the ratio of the volume occupied by the block to the
value of a monomer reference volume.  Let \f$ N_{ma} \f$ denote the
length of block \f$ m \f$ of polymer species \f$ a \f$.

\section scft_propagator_polymer_sec Propagator Functions (Thread Model)

This section discusses the use of "propagator" functions to describe the
statistical mechanics of individual polymers in an ideal gas reference
system. For concreteness, this discussion uses a notation and analysis
appropriate to the continuous thread model. The corresponding discussion
for the bead model would be similar in many respects, but in not
presented here.

\subsection scft_propagator_propagator_sub Propagator Functions

For each block, we define two "propagator" functions, denoted generically
by \f$ q( \textbf{r}, s) \f$. Each propagator is associated with one of
the two directions in which the block may be traversed.  Each propagator
is said to originate at one of the two vertices to which the associated
block is attached and terminate at the other. The contour variable
parameter \f$ s \f$ is defined for each propagator \f$ q(\textbf{r},s) \f$
such that \f$ s = 0 \f$ at the vertex at which the propagator originates
and \f$ s = N \f$ at the vertex at which the propagator terminates, where
\f$ N \f$ is the contour length of the block. When we need to distinguish
between the propagators associated with a single block, we will refer to
them instead as \f$ q \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$, though the choice of
which is referred to as \f$ q \f$ and which \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$ is
arbitrary.

For each vertex that is connected to \f$ M \f$ blocks, there are \f$ M \f$
propagators that originate at that vertex, which we refer to the "outgoing"
propagators for that vertex, and \f$ M \f$ propagators that terminate at
that vertex, which we refer to as "incoming" propagators. In what follows,
we refer to a vertex with \f$ M \geq 2 \f$ attached blocks as an M-fold
junction, and a vertex with only one attached block (\f$ M = 1 \f$ as a
chain end.

It is useful for some purposes to imagine a process in which we divide
a block of length \f$ N \f$ into two blocks of length \f$ s \f$ and
\f$ N - s \f$ by adding a "virtual" two-fold junction at some coordinate
\f$ s \f$ within the block. This yields a two-fold vertex that is
connected to two blocks of the same monomer type. The insertion or
removal of this type of virtual vertex modifies the graph that we use
to describe a polymer but does change our physical description of the
underlying molecule.

\subsection scft_propagator_mde_sub Modified Diffusion Equation (MDE)

Each propagator \f$ q(\textbf{r}, s) \f$ associated with a block of
monomer type \f$ i \f$ can be shown to obey a partial differential
equation (PDE) \anchor scft_propagator_mde_eqn
\f[
  \frac{\partial q}{\partial s} =  -H_{i} q
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (4)
\f]
in which \f$ H_{i} \f$ is a linear differential operator whose
action on any function \f$ f({\bf r}) \f$ of position is defined to
be \anchor scft_propagator_hop_eqn
\f[
  H_{i} f({\bf r})
  = - \frac{b_{i}^{2}}{6} \nabla^{2} f({\bf r})
    + w_{i}(\textbf{r}) f({\bf r})
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (5)
\f]
in which \f$ b_{i} \f$ is the statistical segment length for monomers
of type \f$ i \f$ , and \f$ w_{i}\f$ is the dimensionless SCF chemical
potential field for such monomers.  We refer to this PDE as the
modified diffusion equation (MDE). Numerical solution of the MDE is
the most computationally expensive operation in any SCFT program.

<em> Initial Conditions </em>:

The initial condition for propagators that originate at a chain end
requires that
\f[
   q(\textbf{r},s=0) = 1
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (6)
\f]
for all \f$ \textbf{r} \f$ in the relevant spatial domain.

The initial value \f$ q(\textbf{r}, s=0) \f$ of an outgoing propagator
that originates at an \f$ M \f$-fold junction is given by a pointwise
product of values of the \f$ M - 1 \f$ incoming propagators for that
junction, excluding the incoming propagator associated with the same
block as that of the outgoing propagator of interest.

Suppose we denote the outgoing propagators associated with the M blocks
attached to an M-fold junction \f$ q_{m}({\bf r}, s) \f$, and denote
the final or "tail" values of the M incoming propagators
by \f$ q^{\dagger}_{a}({\bf r}) \f$, where \f$ m = 0, \ldots, M-1 \f$
is an integer identifer for the attached blocks. The initial condition
for any propagator that originates at this vertex is then
\f[
   q_{m}({\bf r}, 0) = \prod_{n \neq m}
   q^{\dagger}_{n}({\bf r})
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (7)
\f]
where the product over \f$ n \f$ is taken over all \f$ [0, \ldots, M-1] \f$
except the value \f$ n = m \f$ corresponding to the block associated
with the outgoing propagator of interest.

In the simple case of a linear polymer, for which every junction connects
two blocks, the initial condition for the each outgoing propagator that
originates at a junction is simply equal to the final value of the
incoming propagator associated with the other block that is attached to
that junction. Consider a linear polymer with \f$ B \f$ blocks in which
blocks and vertices are indexed sequentially by their position relative
to one end of the chain.  This is the indexing scheme for blocks and
vertices that is used internally by PSCF for polymers that are assigned
a linear type parameter value within the parameter file, which is the
default parameter value. Let \f$ N_{m} \f$ denote the length of block
\f$ m \f$, which connects vertices \f$ m \f$ and \f$ m + 1 \f$.  Let
\f$ q_{m} \f$ denotes the propagator in block \f$ m \f$ that originates
at vertex \f$ m \f$, and let \f$ q_{m}^{\dagger} \f$ denote the
propagator in block \f$ m \f$ that originates at vertex \f$ m + 1 \f$.
With this notation,
\f[
   q_{m+1}(\textbf{r},0) = q_{m}(\textbf{r}, N_{a})
\f]
and
\f[
   q_{m}^{\dagger}(\textbf{r},0)
    = q_{m+1}^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N_{m+1})
\f]
for  \f$ m = 0, \ldots, B - 2 \f$.

As an example of a branched polymer, consider the case of a three armed
star containing blocks with indices 0, 1 and 2 that are all connected at
a central junction.  Let \f$ N_{m} \f$ denote the length of block
\f$ m \f$.  Let \f$ q_{m}(\textbf{r},s) \f$ denote the propagator in
block \f$ m \f$ that originates at the central junction, and let
\f$ q_{m}^{\dagger}(\textbf{r},s) \f$ denote the propagator in block
\f$ m \f$ that originates at a free end and terminates at the
central junction. The initial condition for propagators that originate
at chain ends requires that \f$ q_{m}^{\dagger}(\textbf{r},s=0) = 1 \f$
for all \f$ \textbf{r} \f$ and all \f$ m \f$. The initial condition for
the outgoing propagator in block 0 that originates at the central vertex
is then given by, for example, by
\f[
    q_{0}(\textbf{r}, s=0) =
    q_{1}^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N_1)
    q_{2}^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N_2) \quad.
    \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (8)
\f]
Initial conditions for \f$ q_{1} \f$ and \f$ q_{2} \f$ are analogous.

<em> Boundary Conditions </em>:

In the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg programs, the MDE for propagators in a
periodic structure is solved within a single unit cell, subject to
periodic boundary conditions.

In the pscf_r1d program, the MDE for each propagator \f$ q(x,s) \f$
is solved within a range of values of the single spatial coordinate
\f$ x \f$, where \f$ x \f$ may represent either a Cartesian normal
coordinate for a system with the geometry of a flat film or a radial
coordinate for a system with cylindrical or spherical symmetry.
In the case of a problem that is defined in cylindrical or
spherical coordinates with a domain that includes \f$ x = 0 \f$, we
must require that \f$ \partial q/\partial x = 0 \f$ at \f$ x = 0 \f$
in order to avoid introducing gradient discontinuities at the
origin. In most foreseeable applications of this program, one or
both of the limits of the computational domain is intended to
represent a point that lies deep within homogeneous region, such
as bulk region surrounding a micelle or a bulk phase far from an
interface. In these cases, it is also appropriate to set
\f$ \partial q/\partial x = 0 \f$ at these boundaries. In light of
these considerations, the pscf_r1d program is designed to apply
von Neumann boundary conditions requiring that
\f$ \partial q/\partial x = 0 \f$ at both upper and lower limiting
values of \f$ x \f$.

\subsection scft_propagator_rhou_sub Monomer Probability Densities (Unnormalized)

Values of propagator functions that are obtained by solving the modified
diffusion equation can be used to compute the probability of finding any
point on a polymer chain within an ideal gas reference system at any
at specified position within the relevant spatial domain: The probability
per unit volume of finding a specified vertex of a polymer at a specific
local \f$ {\bf r} \f$ can be shown to be proportional to the product of the
incoming propagators for that vertex, at the the position \f$ {\bf r} \f$.

This relationship applies to an optional or "virtual" 2-fold vertex
inserted at any point within any block of an acyclic branched polymer,
as well as to vertices that are necessary to describe the polymer topology.
Let \f$ \rho({\bf r}, s) \f$  denote the probability density per unit
volume that a monomer or point with a contour coordinate \f$ s \f$ within
a specified block of length \f$ N \f$ will be found within an infinitesimal
volume centered at a position \f$ {\bf r} \f$.  This quantity is given
to within a constant of proportionality by
\anchor scft_propagator_rhou_eqn
\f[
   \rho({\bf r}, s) \propto
   q({\bf r}, s) q^{\dagger}({\bf r}, N-s) \quad,
    \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (9)
\f]
Here, \f$ q({\bf r}, s) \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger}({\bf r}, N - s) \f$ are
the two propagators associated with that block. These are also the two
incoming propagators associated with a virtual vertex introduced at
point \f$ s \f$ within the block. The two propagators in this product
are evaluated at contour coordinates that correspond to the same point
within the block, but are each evaluated at a value \f$ s \f$ or
\f$ N - s \f$ that corresponds to the contour distance measured from
the vertex at which that propagator originates. The more general
expression for the probability density for the position of an M-fold
vertex involves the product of the M incoming propagators, all
evaluated at the same position \f$ {\bf r} \f$, and at contour coordinate
values that correspond to the lengths of the associated blocks.

We show below that the constant of proportionality that relates the
probability density for a vertex to the product of incoming propagators
is the same for every vertex in a polymer, as well as for any possible
virtual vertex (i.e., any monomer within any block of that polymer).

\subsection scft_propagator_products_sub Theorems about Propagator Products

We may use the MDE and its associated initial and boundary conditions
to prove the following series of theorems about products of incoming
propagators associated with any vertex in a polymer.

\anchor scft_propagator_theorem1
<b> Theorem A.1 </b> :
Suppose \f$ q({\bf r}, s) q^{\dagger}({\bf r},N-s) \f$ is the product
of two propagators defined within a single block of length \f$ N \f$,
defined such that \f$ q \f$ originates at the vertex with coordinate
\f$ s = 0 \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$ originates at the vertex with
coordinate \f$ s = N \f$.  The value of this product at \f$ s = 0 \f$
is equal to the product of all the incoming propagators at the vertex
with coordinate \f$ s = 0 \f$, and the value of this product at
\f$ s = N \f$ is equal to the product of all incoming propagators at
the vertex with coordinate \f$ s = N \f$.

<em> Proof </em>:
First consider the value at \f$ s = 0 \f$ for \f$ M > 1 \f$.
The initial condition for \f$ q \f$ at \f$ s = 0 \f$ for an M-fold
junction with \f$ M > 1 \f$ requires that \f$ q({\bf r}, s = 0) \f$
is equal to the product of the final values of incoming propagators
associated with all *other* blocks that are attached to that vertex
, excluding the incoming propagator associated with the block along
which \f$ q \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$ are defined. It follows
immediately that the value of the product
\f$ q({\bf r},s) q^{\dagger}({\bf r},N-s) \f$ at \f$ s = 0 \f$ is
thus equal to the product of all incoming propagators at that vertex,
including the incoming propagator \f$ q^{\dagger}({\bf r}, N-s)\f$
associated with the block of interest.

Next consider the case in which the vertex at \f$ s = 0 \f$ is a chain
end, with \f$ M = 1 \f$.  In this case, \f$ q(\textbf{r}, s) = 1 \f$,
and so the product \f$ q q^{\dagger} \f$ simply yields the value of
the single incoming propagagator \f$ q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N) \f$.
For \f$ M = 1 \f$, \f$ q^{\dagger}({\bf r}, N) \f$ is equal to the
product of incoming propagators.

The corresponding statement about the value of \f$ q q^{\dagger} \f$
at \f$ s = N \f$ is obtained by completely analogous reasoning, with
the roles of \f$ q \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$ reversed.

\anchor scft_propagator_theorem2
<b> Theorem A.2 </b>: Let \f$ I(s) \f$ represent the spatial integral
\f[
   I(s) \equiv
   \int d\textbf{r} \; q (\textbf{r},s) q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N-s)
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (10)
\f]
of a product of two propagators that are both defined within a block
of length \f$ N \f$, where \f$ s \f$ is the contour variable for a
point within that block.
If the propagators \f$ q \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$ obey a modified
diffusion equation within some domain, subject to periodic, Dirichlet,
von Neumann or Robin boundary conditions, then the value of
\f$ I(s) \f$ is independent of contour coordinate \f$ s \f$ for all
\f$ s \in [0,N] \f$.

<em> Proof </em>:
Note that
\f{eqnarray*}
  \frac{dI(s)}{ds} & = & \frac{d}{ds}
  \int d\textbf{r} \;
  q (\textbf{r},s) q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N - s) \\
  & = &
  \int d\textbf{r} \;  \left \{
  \frac{\partial q (\textbf{r},s)}{\partial s}
  q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N - s )
  - q (\textbf{r},s)
  \frac{\partial q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N-s)}{\partial s}
  \right \} \\
  & = &
  -\int d\textbf{r} \;  \left \{
  [ \hat{H} q (\textbf{r},s) ] q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N-s)
  - q (\textbf{r},s) [\hat{H} q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N-s)]
  \right \}
  \quad.
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (11)
\f}
We may then use the fact that the operator \f$ \hat{H} \f$ (like the
analogous quantum mechanical Hamiltonian operator for a particle in a
potential) can be shown to be a self-adjoint differential operator for
any of the boundary conditions given in the theorem statement to show
that
\f[
  \int d\textbf{r} \;
  [\hat{H} q (\textbf{r},s) ] q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N-s)
  =
  \int d\textbf{r} \;
  q (\textbf{r},s) [\hat{H} q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N-s) ] \quad,
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (12)
\f]
implying a cancellation of the two terms in the integrand of the last
expression for \f$ dI(s)/ds \f$. We thereby show that
\f[
  dI(s)/ds = 0
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (13)
\f]
for all \f$ s \in [0,N] \f$, implying that \f$ I(s) \f$ is independent
of \f$ s \f$ within each block.

\anchor scft_propagator_theorem3
<b> Theorem A.3 </b>: The value of the spatial integral of the product
of incoming propagators associated with a vertex of an acyclic branched
polymer has the same value for every vertex in the polymer, including
any virtual 2-fold vertex inserted into any block of the polymer.

<em> Proof </em>:
This follows immediately from the combination of the preceding two
theorems:  \ref scft_propagator_theorem2 "Theorem A.2" shows that the
value of the spatial integral \f$ I(s) \f$ of the product of incoming
propagators associated with a virtual 2-fold vertex inserted into any
block of a polymer is independent of the coordinate \f$ s \f$ within the
block. Thereom \ref scft_propagator_theorem1 "Theorem A.1" then implies
that the integral of the product of incoming propagators must also have
the same value for any vertices that are directly connected by a block.
Because any two distinct vertices in an acyclic branched polymer are
connected by a unique path of one or more blocks, this implies that
the value of this spatial integral must be the same for every vertex
in any acyclic branched polymer. Theorem A.2 also implies that this
applies to virtual 2-fold vertices that we insert at any point within
any block.

\subsection scft_propagator_bigQ_sub Molecular Partition Function

Let \f$ Q_{a} \f$ denote a normalized single-chain partition function
for a polymer molecule of species \f$ a \f$ within an ideal gas reference
system that is subjected to a specified set of monomer potential fields.
We define the value of \f$ Q_{a} \f$ to be equal to the spatial average
(i.e., the integral over the entire spatial domain of the problem
divided by the volume of this domain) of the product of the values of
the incoming propagators evaluated at any vertex of the polymer. The
statement of theorem A.3 implies that we should get the same value for
\f$ Q_{a} \f$ by evaluating it using any vertex in a polymer of species
\f$ a \f$. Different values for \f$ Q_{a} \f$ are, however, generally
obtained for different species within a mixture.

A particularly compact expression for this quantity may be obtained
by evaluating the incoming propagator at any chain end, for which
the vertex is attached to a single block of length \f$ N \f$. In this
case, the above definition yields
\anchor scft_propagator_Qend_eqn
\f[
   Q_{a} = \frac{1}{V}
   \int \! d\textbf{r} \; q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N)
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (14)
\f]
where \f$ q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r}, N) \f$ denotes the final value of
the single incoming propagator at that chain end.  Here, the integral
is taken over the entire spatial domain for the problem of interest,
while \f$ V \f$ is the generalized volume of that domain. In the case
of a periodic structure, the required spatial average may be evaluated
by evaluating the integral over one crystallographic unit cell and
then dividing by the generalized volume of that unit cell  (i.e., the
volume per unit cell for a 3D crystal, area per 2D unit cell for a 2D
structure such as a hexagonal cylinder phase, or the length per unit
cell in a 1D lamellar phase).

Another potentially useful expression for \f$ Q_{a} \f$ may be obtained
by considering a virtual 2-fold vertex inserted at contour coordinate
\f$ s \f$ within any block \f$ m \f$ of length \f$ N_{ma} \f$ of
polymer species \f$ a \f$ and using the result of theorem A.3 to show
that \anchor scft_propagator_Q2vertex
\f[
   Q_{a} = \frac{1}{V}
   \int d\textbf{r} \; q (\textbf{r},s) q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r},N-s)
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (15)
\f]
where \f$ N \f$ is the length of the chosen block, and where theorem
A.3 guarantees that the value of the RHS of this equation is actually
independent of the choice of values for \f$ s \f$ and block index
\f$ m \f$.

\subsection scft_propagator_rho_sub Monomer Probability Densities (Normalized)

We can use the above definition of \f$ Q_{a} \f$ to write an explicit
expression for the absolute probability density for the location of
any specified monomer within an ideal gas reference system.  Let
\f$ \rho_{m a}({\bf r}, s) \f$ denote the probability density for the
position \f$ {\bf r} \f$ of a monomer with contour coordinate \f$ s \f$
within block \f$ m \f$ of a single polymer of species \f$ a \f$ within
a system that contains only a single polymer of this species, with no
non-bonded pair interactions.  By combining our previous
\ref scft_propagator_rhou_eqn "proportionality", which gives this
quantity to within a constant, with the requirement that integral of
this probability density over the problem domain must equal unity, we
find that
\f[
   \rho_{ma}({\bf r}, s) = \frac{1}{I_{ia}(s)}
   q({\bf r}, s) q^{\dagger}({\bf r}, N_{ia} - s) \quad,
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (16)
\f]
in which \f$ q \f$ and \f$ q^{\dagger} \f$ are the two propagators
associated with the block of interest, \f$ N_{ma} \f$ is the length of
this block, and \f$ I_{ma}(s) \f$ is the spatial integral defined in
the statement of \ref scft_propagator_theorem2 "theorem A.2", defined
here for monomer with contour coordinate \f$ s \f$ within block
\f$ m \f$ of polymer species \f$ a \f$.
Using the expression given \ref scft_propagator_Q2vertex "above" to
relate \f$ Q_{a} \f$ and \f$ I_{ma}(s) \f$, we find that
\anchor scft_propagator_rhoia_eqn
\f[
   \rho_{ma} ({\bf r}, s) = \frac{1}{V Q_{a}}
   q({\bf r}, s) q^{\dagger}({\bf r}, N-s)
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (17)
\f]
for any choice of block index \f$ m \f$ and contour coordinate \f$ s \f$.

\subsection scft_propagator_phi_sub Block Volume Fractions

Consider a system that contains \f$ M_{a} \f$ chains of polymer species
\f$ a \f$ within a total volume \f$ V \f$. The volume occupied by each
such polymer is \f$ v N_{a} \f$, where \f$ N_{a} \f$ is the total
length of polymer species \f$ a \f$, given by the sum of the length of
all of the blocks of that polymer. Let \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ denote
the average overall volume fraction occupied by molecule species \f$ a \f$
within the mixture. This quantity is given by a ratio
\f[
   \overline{\phi}_{a} = \frac{M_{a} N_{a} v}{V}
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (18)
\f]
where the numerator of the RHS is the total volume occupied by
\f$ M_{a} \f$ such chains.

Let \f$ \phi_{ma}(\textbf{r}) \f$ denote the local volume fraction
of monomers belonging to block \f$ m \f$ of polymer species
\f$ a \f$.  This quantity is related to the probability density
\f$ \rho_{ia}({\bf r},s) \f$ defined
\ref scft_propagator_rhoia_eqn "above" by
\f[
   \phi_{ia}({\bf r}) = v M_{a}
   \int\limits_{0}^{N_{ma}} \! ds \; \rho_{ma}({\bf r}, s) \quad,
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (19)
\f]
where \f$ M_{a} \f$ is the total number of chains of species \f$ a \f$
and \f$ N_{ma} \f$ is the length of the relevant block.  Rewriting
\f$ v M_{a} \f$ in terms of \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ then yields
\anchor scft_propagator_phiia_eqn
\f[
   \phi_{ma}(\textbf{r}) =
   \frac{\overline{\phi}_{i}}{N_{a}Q_{a}}
   \int\limits_{0}^{N_{ia}} \! ds \;
    q(\textbf{r},s) q^{\dagger}(\textbf{r},s)
  \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (20)
\f]
where \f$ N_{a} \f$ is the total contour length of all of the blocks
of polymer species \f$ a \f$.

The total volume fraction \f$ \phi_{i}(\textbf{r}) \f$ for each
monomer type \f$ i \f$ is simply the sum of volume fractions for
all polymer blocks and all solvent species that have been assigned
a monomer type index \f$ i \f$ .

\section scft_propagator_solvent_sec Solvent species

Each solvent species \f$ a \f$ is associated with a specific monomer type
\f$ i \f$ and a dimensionless "size" parameter. The size parameter for a
solvent is a measure of volume closely analogous to the length parameter
for a block in a block polymer, and is equal to the ratio of the volume
per molecule occupied by the solvent to the monomer reference volume. In
light of this analogy, we denote the "size" of solvent \f$ a\f$ here by
\f$ N_{a} \f$.

In the single molecule problem for solvent species, the free energy
penalty for a solvent molecule of monomer type \f$ i \f$ to be located
at position \f$ \textbf{r} \f$ is given by
\f$ k_{B}T N_{a} w_{i}(\textbf{r}) \f$ .
This implies the existence of a solvent volume fraction field
\f$ \phi_{a}(\textbf{r}) \propto \exp[-N_{a}w_{i}(\textbf{r})] \f$ .

We define a normalized molecular partition function \f$ Q_{a} \f$ for
solvent species \f$ a \f$ that is given by an integral
\f[
   Q_{a} = \frac{1}{V}
   \int \! d\textbf{r} \; \exp (-N_{a}w_{i}(\textbf{r}) )
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (21)
\f]
for all \f$ a = P, \ldots, P + S - 1 \f$ .
The local volume fraction \f$ \phi_{a}(\textbf{r}) \f$ for solvent
species \f$ a \f$ is then given by a ratio
\f[
   \phi_{a}(\textbf{r}) =
   \frac{\overline{\phi}_{a}}{Q_{a}}
   \exp(-N_{a}w_{i}(\textbf{r}))
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (22)
\f]
in which \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ is the overall volume fraction
of solvent species \f$ a \f$ within the mixture. Note that the
prefactor in this expression is chosen to guarantee that the spatial
average of \f$ \phi_{a}(\textbf{r}) \f$ (the integral divided by total
volume) is given by the parameter \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$.

\section scft_propagator_ensemble_sec Open and closed ensembles

PSCF allows the user to choose either an open or closed statistical
ensemble for each molecular species in the mixture, by allowing a
user to enter a value for either the overall volume fraction for
that species, denoted here by \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$, or a
value for the chemical potential, denoted here by \f$ \mu_{a} \f$.

To use an open ensemble for a particular species in SCFT, one uses an
open (i.e., grand-canonical) ensemble to treat the behavior of that
species when considering the hypothetical ideal gas of non-interacting
molecules subjected to a potential. Use of a grand-canonical
ensemble to describe to a gas of molecules of species \f$ a \f$
yields an overall concentration for that species and a corresponding
overall volume fraction \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ that are both
proportional to the product \f$ e^{\mu_{a}/k_{B} T} Q_{a} \f$. The
value of the constants of proportionality in the expressions depends
upon arbitrary choices of convention for normalizing volume used to
relate the configurational integral for a single chain to a
dimensionless partition function and a choice of reference state used
to define the chemical potential. In the equations used in PSCF, we
set \anchor scft_propagator_phiofmu_eqn
\f[
   \overline{\phi}_{a} = e^{\mu_{a}/k_{B} T} Q_{a} \quad,
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (23)
\f]
as a matter of convention.

Calculations of the local volume fractions for monomers within a
specific block of a block copolymer and for the local volume fraction
of a solvent species are thus performed for molecular species that
are treated in an open ensemble, by simply replacing the parameter
\f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ by the above expression in all expressions
that involve \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$.

The value of the chemical potential is computed for any species that
is treated in a closed ensemble (i.e., any species for which a volume
fraction is specified in the parameter file) by computing
\anchor scft_propagator_mu_eqn
\f[
   \frac{\mu_{a}}{k_{B}T }
   = \ln \left ( \overline{\phi}_{a} / Q_{a}  \right )
   \quad,
   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad (24)
\f]
after computing \f$ Q_{a} \f$.

Values of species chemical potentials in both input and output files
used by PSCF, which are denoted indicated the label "mu", are given in
thermal energy units, and thus are correspond to values of the ratio
\f$ \mu_{a}/k_{B}T \f$.

\section scft_propagator_inputs_sec Overview: SCFT Working equations

The above discussion provide a complete set of working equations for SCFT of
an incompressible liquid in the form implemented in PSCF, for calculations in
a fixed spatial domain.  We simplify the discussion by restricting ourselves
here to calculation that are carried out in a fixed one-dimensional or periodic
spatial domain. Extension to SCFT calculations in a periodic unit cell with
flexible parameters, as performed by pscf_rpc or pscf_rpg programs, would
require an additional set of working equations to impose a requirement that
the free energy density be minimized with respect to  variation of the unit
cell parameters.  The working equations for a PSCF SCFT calculation in a fixed
spatial domain are:

   - The modified diffusion equation
     [Eqs. (4) and (5)]

   - A definition of \f$ Q_{a} \f$ for each species
     [Eq. (14) for polymers and Eq. (21) for solvents]

   - The relationship between \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ and
     \f$ \mu_{a} \f$
     [Eq. (23) or (24)]

   - Expressions for block and solvent monomer volume fractions
     [Eq. (20) for a polymer block and (22) for a solvent]

   - Self-consistent field condition
     [Eq. (3)]

   - Incompressibility constraint
     [Eq. (2)]

   - Expression for non-dimensional Helmholtz free energy per monomer,
     \f$ f \f$, given \ref scft_thermo_helmholtz_sec "here".

   - Expression for non-dimensional pressure \f$ p \f$, or the
     associated non-dimensional grand-canonical free energy, given
     \ref scft_thermo_pressure_sec "here".

The key outputs from solution of this set of equations are:

  - A converged monomer chemical potential field
    \f$ w_{i}({\bf r}) \f$ ("w field") for each monomer type
    \f$ i = 0, \ldots, C-1 \f$

  - A converged monomer concentration field ("c field")
    \f$ \phi_{i}({\bf r}) \f$ for each monomer type \f$ i \f$

  - A chemical potential \f$ \mu_{a} \f$ or overall volume
    fraction \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$ for each polymer and solvent
    species \f$ a \f$ (depending on whether \f$ \overline{\phi}_{a} \f$
    or \f$ \mu_{a} \f$ was provided as an input parameter for that
    species)

  - Values for non-dimensionalized Helmholtz free energy
    \f$ f \f$ and pressure \f$ p \f$.

The required inputs to such an SCFT calculation are:

  - A statistical segment length for each monomer type

  - A description of each block polymer type as a list of
    block lengths and vertex indices for attached vertices

  - A size (ratio of molecular volume to monomer reference volume)
    for each solvent species

  - Values for all elements of the symmetric matrix of Flory-Huggins
    interaction parameters

  - A description of the spatial domain and boundary conditions

\section scft_propagator_vMonomer_sec Monomer reference volume

It is straightforward to confirm that the monomer reference volume
\f$ v \f$ does not appear as an input parameter in any of the final
working equations enumerated above. This parameter is thus not a
required input parameter for an SCFT calculation.

PSCF allows the user to optionally input a value for \f$ v \f$ in
the parameter file (labelled as "vMonomer"), but this value is not
used in the solution of SCFT equations. The option of entering a
value is provided because:

  - A value for \f$ v \f$ is required as an input to field
    theoretic simulations that can be performed by pscf_rpc
    and pscf_rpg.

  - A value for \f$ v \f$ is required to compute some physical
    quantities that can be inferred from solution of a SCFT
    problem.

The role of the monomer volume in PS-FTS simulation is discussed
in the next \ref psfts_page "manual section". We discuss the use
of monomer volume in the interpretation of SCFT results below.

A value for \f$ v \f$ is required in the analysis of SCFT results
to compute some physical properties that require knowledge of the
number of monomers or molecules that can pack into a region of
space.  In SCFT treatments of periodic structures formed by a
block polymer material, such properties include the number of
molecules per unit cell and the total free energy per unit cell.
For calculations that can be performed by pscf_1d code, such
properties include, for example, the aggregation number and
total excess free energy of a spherical micelle, the interfacial
tension of an interface, and the excess concentration per area
(adsorbed molecules per area) of a species that absorbs to an
interface.  The pscf_r1d program provides specialized commands
that can compute several of these properties of micelles and
interfaces, but that require a value for \f$ v \f$ to be provided
in the parameter file.

